NNAAMMEE
     ps - print process status

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppss [-aaddffllmmnnrrttwwxx] [-cc _s_y_s_t_e_m] [-kk _m_e_m]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ppss prints one line of status for each process, reading the
     running kernel's process table out of _m_e_m by way of the
     namelist in _s_y_s_t_e_m. Normally ppss prints only processes at-
     tached to the caller's own controlling terminal; the fields
     always printed are TTTTYY (controlling terminal, or ??  if
     none) and PPIIDD.

     -aa   Print processes attached to any terminal, not only the
          caller's.

     -cc _s_y_s_t_e_m
          Take the kernel namelist from _s_y_s_t_e_m instead of the
          default, /ccoohheerreenntt.

     -dd   Precede each line with the process's queue-entry ad-
          dress, in octal.  This option is implemented by this ppss
          but is not listed in the quick-help text above; see
          NOTES.

     -ff   Print a - placeholder in fields that would otherwise be
          blank, so field-oriented filters can split the output
          uniformly.

     -kk _m_e_m
          Take the memory image from _m_e_m instead of the default,
          /ddeevv/mmeemm.

     -ll   Long format: also print PPPPIIDD, UUIIDD, process size in K,
          the flag word, the run state, and, for a sleeping
          process, the event it is waiting on.

     -mm   Also print the scheduling fields CCVVAALL,  SSVVAALL,  IIVVAALL,
          and  the process's current CPU schedule value, swap
          schedule value, importance value, and response value.

     -nn   Suppress the header line.

     -rr   Print the real size of the process, including its user
          and auxiliary segments (normally excluded, since those
          are shared with other processes of the same user).

     -tt   Also print the elapsed CPU time fields UUTTIIMMEE and SSTTIIMMEE.

     -ww   Wide format: use 132 columns for the command line in-
          stead of 80.

     -xx   Also print processes with no controlling terminal.



